00:00:34.320 --> 00:00:36.930
- Actually, I knew, I think
originally it was written

00:00:36.930 --> 00:00:38.220
sort of a little more smart ass,

00:00:38.220 --> 00:00:41.520
and kind of calling Eugene's
character on getting

00:00:41.520 --> 00:00:42.420
us in the situation

00:00:42.420 --> 00:00:43.505
and giving him a hard time.

00:00:43.505 --> 00:00:44.550
- Yeah.
- And very funny.

00:00:44.550 --> 00:00:46.770
But I just thought I'd
seen that done so well

00:00:46.770 --> 00:00:48.030
by other people that I was getting...

00:00:48.030 --> 00:00:49.950
And so I wanted to be more feminine.

00:00:49.950 --> 00:00:51.445
- Well, it's been done
by you in "Beetlejuice,"

00:00:51.445 --> 00:00:52.392
by the way. (laughs)

00:00:52.392 --> 00:00:53.225
- Oh, yeah, that's true.
- The mother was

00:00:53.225 --> 00:00:54.058
kind of a smart ass.

00:00:54.058 --> 00:00:55.620
I love that.
- Yes, but when I argue

00:00:55.620 --> 00:00:57.060
I feel like I get very harsh and stuff,

00:00:57.060 --> 00:00:58.091
and I just wanted
- You get awesome.

00:00:58.091 --> 00:00:58.924
- to be feminine.

00:00:58.924 --> 00:01:00.900
But I know a woman who is so feminine,

00:01:00.900 --> 00:01:02.520
and I thought that what a great way to go.

00:01:02.520 --> 00:01:05.984
Just be soft and keep
the mouth moving softly.

00:01:05.984 --> 00:01:07.515
(all laugh)

00:01:07.515 --> 00:01:09.651
Yeah. Just. (laughs)

00:01:09.651 --> 00:01:10.709
(audience claps and cheers)

00:01:10.709 --> 00:01:12.450
- It's surreal. It's surreal.

00:01:12.450 --> 00:01:13.983
- So incredible.

00:01:13.983 --> 00:01:16.080
I've been very excited about this moment.

00:01:16.080 --> 00:01:18.750
Anyway, do you ever take
inspiration from like real people

00:01:18.750 --> 00:01:20.460
that you know for characters?

00:01:20.460 --> 00:01:21.780
- You know, sometimes things are just

00:01:21.780 --> 00:01:23.760
sort of like obvious and make sense.

00:01:23.760 --> 00:01:25.350
- Yeah.
- And there was one time

00:01:25.350 --> 00:01:27.000
I was doing a movie and yeah, no,

00:01:27.000 --> 00:01:29.310
someone did something quite awful,

00:01:29.310 --> 00:01:32.250
and their walk just made sense for this

00:01:32.250 --> 00:01:34.290
character that I played in "The Help."

00:01:34.290 --> 00:01:35.819
And so-
- Oh my God,

00:01:35.819 --> 00:01:36.780
what a great film!

00:01:36.780 --> 00:01:38.650
Y'all have been in so many great films.

00:01:38.650 --> 00:01:41.400
- So it was, yeah, no, it was a walk.

00:01:41.400 --> 00:01:42.233
- Yeah.
- And it was,

00:01:42.233 --> 00:01:43.740
- Wow.
- And everyone who knew

00:01:43.740 --> 00:01:45.660
this person knew that it was her.

00:01:45.660 --> 00:01:47.973
Oh, I love the hidden tokens.
- That's great.

00:01:47.973 --> 00:01:49.410
- Wow, that's amazing.
- Yes.

00:01:49.410 --> 00:01:50.910
- Well, speaking of walking, you got a lot

00:01:50.910 --> 00:01:52.800
of your like physical
comedy from your dad, right?

00:01:52.800 --> 00:01:53.633
- I did, yeah.

00:01:53.633 --> 00:01:54.710
- It was some kind of walk, right?

00:01:54.710 --> 00:01:57.510
- Yeah, I did a walk in
the end of "Best in Show"

00:01:57.510 --> 00:02:00.275
where I've hurt my knee. (laughs)

00:02:00.275 --> 00:02:02.520
And the day before we, you
know, it was improvised movie,

00:02:02.520 --> 00:02:03.630
but it was all in the script.

00:02:03.630 --> 00:02:05.400
Everything, all the
outline except dialogue.

00:02:05.400 --> 00:02:07.417
And the night before Chris said,

00:02:07.417 --> 00:02:08.340
"What are we gonna do when?"

00:02:08.340 --> 00:02:09.720
Chris Guest said, "What are
you gonna do when you fall?"

00:02:09.720 --> 00:02:12.960
I said, "There's this walk my
dad would do. Could I try?"

00:02:12.960 --> 00:02:14.040
He's, "Let me see it."

00:02:14.040 --> 00:02:15.791
And he just said, "Oh, yes."

00:02:15.791 --> 00:02:16.710
(all laugh)

00:02:16.710 --> 00:02:17.543
Yeah, so we did.

00:02:17.543 --> 00:02:19.530
My dad would walk ahead
of us going into church

00:02:19.530 --> 00:02:20.580
or going into a mall or something.

00:02:20.580 --> 00:02:23.640
We'd all laugh and
everyone of the seven kids

00:02:23.640 --> 00:02:25.110
in our family does the walk.

00:02:25.110 --> 00:02:27.040
But I got to do it in a movie. (laughs)

00:02:27.040 --> 00:02:27.900
(audience laughs)

00:02:27.900 --> 00:02:28.884
- Yeah, you got to be the first.

00:02:28.884 --> 00:02:30.819
That's a lot of siblings.

00:02:30.819 --> 00:02:32.310
- [Catherine] Yeah, six. Six be a...

00:02:32.310 --> 00:02:33.143
Yeah.
- That's where

00:02:33.143 --> 00:02:34.020
the humor comes in.

00:02:34.020 --> 00:02:35.130
- Catherine O'Hara.

00:02:35.130 --> 00:02:36.600
- You have to, you have to laugh.

00:02:36.600 --> 00:02:37.433
- We do.

00:02:37.433 --> 00:02:38.880
My mom and dad laughed every day.

00:02:38.880 --> 00:02:40.860
It was so great and sexy.
- Yeah.

00:02:40.860 --> 00:02:42.840
Oh, I agree. I agree.
- Yeah.

00:02:42.840 --> 00:02:43.830
- Well, speaking of family,

00:02:43.830 --> 00:02:45.570
Bryce shared this old photo,

00:02:45.570 --> 00:02:46.403
so we're gonna put that.

00:02:46.403 --> 00:02:47.280
Look, okay.

00:02:47.280 --> 00:02:48.510
Where are you there?

00:02:48.510 --> 00:02:49.530
- [Bryce] You know, I don't even like

00:02:49.530 --> 00:02:51.000
really know necessarily.

00:02:51.000 --> 00:02:52.302
- But you're winking at us. (laughs)

00:02:52.302 --> 00:02:53.135
- Oh, am I?

00:02:53.135 --> 00:02:54.180
Oh no, I'm, you know what it is.

00:02:54.180 --> 00:02:55.880
I'm like, I'm very sensitive to sun so.

00:02:55.880 --> 00:02:56.870
- Me too.
- So, yes.

00:02:56.870 --> 00:02:59.571
So it's very likely that I
just couldn't see anything.

00:02:59.571 --> 00:03:00.404
(all laugh)

00:03:00.404 --> 00:03:02.043
- Yeah, but my family,

00:03:02.880 --> 00:03:05.010
we always go on road trips.

00:03:05.010 --> 00:03:06.840
That's always the thing that I would do.

00:03:06.840 --> 00:03:07.950
Kind of like growing up.
- Yeah.

00:03:07.950 --> 00:03:10.560
- And it would always be these road trips.

00:03:10.560 --> 00:03:14.237
And no one enjoyed them
except for my mother.

00:03:14.237 --> 00:03:18.581
- (laughs) Forced family fun.
- Yeah, yeah, yeah, yeah.

00:03:18.581 --> 00:03:20.430
And it was like the second
like the car door was shut,

00:03:20.430 --> 00:03:22.020
we knew that like, there was no escaping

00:03:22.020 --> 00:03:23.430
for at least six and a half hours.

00:03:23.430 --> 00:03:24.297
And so it was just like,

00:03:24.297 --> 00:03:26.010
did you bring the bananas with you?

00:03:26.010 --> 00:03:27.210
Like, it was like very intense.

00:03:27.210 --> 00:03:30.660
But it was, you know, like there were,

00:03:30.660 --> 00:03:33.420
there were definitely a
lot of great memories.

00:03:33.420 --> 00:03:35.310
And then there were a few weird ones.

00:03:35.310 --> 00:03:39.450
One time my parents were
driving from LA to Yosemite

00:03:39.450 --> 00:03:41.700
and they left my 11-year-old brother

00:03:41.700 --> 00:03:44.222
in a Krispy Kreme in Los Angeles.

00:03:44.222 --> 00:03:45.055
(audience laughs)

00:03:45.055 --> 00:03:47.523
And it took them four
hours to figure it out.

00:03:48.544 --> 00:03:49.377
- Oh!
- And they arrived

00:03:49.377 --> 00:03:51.680
in Yosemite.
- Four hours!

00:03:51.680 --> 00:03:52.953
- Four hours.
- That's bad.

00:03:52.953 --> 00:03:55.980
- What kind of therapy has
this one been through? (laughs)

00:03:55.980 --> 00:03:57.891
- I know, I know. Well actually-

00:03:57.891 --> 00:03:59.104
- I feel really good as a mother now.

00:03:59.104 --> 00:04:01.170
(all laugh)

00:04:01.170 --> 00:04:03.780
- It's very "Home Alone,"
actually. (laughs)

00:04:03.780 --> 00:04:05.921
- Totally "Home Alone."
- Leaving the kid.

00:04:05.921 --> 00:04:07.200
- No, that was reasonable.

00:04:07.200 --> 00:04:08.170
That was...

00:04:08.170 --> 00:04:10.013
(all laugh)

00:04:10.013 --> 00:04:11.850
No four hours?
- Four hours.

00:04:11.850 --> 00:04:13.350
- He was in a Krispy Kreme.

00:04:13.350 --> 00:04:15.449
It's a nice place to hang.
- There are worse places.

00:04:15.449 --> 00:04:16.350
- But then it became eight hours,

00:04:16.350 --> 00:04:19.052
because my parents needed
to drive back to get him.

00:04:19.052 --> 00:04:19.885
- Oh.
- Oh.

00:04:19.885 --> 00:04:22.650
- And my mom cried the entire
time, and they show up,

00:04:22.650 --> 00:04:25.560
and my, you know, this is
a little stereotypical,

00:04:25.560 --> 00:04:27.120
so I apologize if I offend anyone.

00:04:27.120 --> 00:04:28.170
He was surrounded by cops

00:04:28.170 --> 00:04:29.640
and they were all eating donuts together.

00:04:29.640 --> 00:04:30.570
He was fine.
(all laugh)

00:04:30.570 --> 00:04:32.370
- It's what it is.
- He was fine.

00:04:32.370 --> 00:04:34.230
- I mean, we all eat
donuts, not just cops.

00:04:34.230 --> 00:04:35.063
They're good.

00:04:35.063 --> 00:04:35.896
- Yes, yes.
- They're good.

00:04:35.896 --> 00:04:36.900
- Especially when you get
there and they're warm.

00:04:36.900 --> 00:04:39.389
I mean, so I've heard.
(all laugh)

00:04:39.389 --> 00:04:40.222
(audience cheers and claps)

00:04:40.222 --> 00:04:42.210
So their new movie is called "Argylle."

00:04:42.210 --> 00:04:45.600
It's in theaters and IMAX
nationwide starting tomorrow.

00:04:45.600 --> 00:04:46.890
So before we get to "Argylle,"

00:04:46.890 --> 00:04:48.360
'cause we, I don't even know how we're

00:04:48.360 --> 00:04:50.070
gonna explain it without spoiling it,

00:04:50.070 --> 00:04:51.030
but before we get to that,

00:04:51.030 --> 00:04:53.507
I heard that there is a
"Beetlejuice 2," possibly.

00:04:53.507 --> 00:04:54.677
- Yeah, there is.
(Kelly gasps)

00:04:54.677 --> 00:04:57.912
- Oh my God.
(audience cheers and claps)

00:04:57.912 --> 00:04:59.430
- Well, I heard that you actually met your

00:04:59.430 --> 00:05:00.887
husband on the set of "Beetlejuice."

00:05:00.887 --> 00:05:01.720
- (laughs) I did.

00:05:01.720 --> 00:05:03.210
Yeah, he was a production designer,

00:05:03.210 --> 00:05:04.920
designed all those wild sets.
- Yeah.

00:05:04.920 --> 00:05:07.200
- And they actually did
redid the whole house

00:05:07.200 --> 00:05:09.270
on the same hill in
Vermont for this second-

00:05:09.270 --> 00:05:10.103
- For the second one?
- Yeah, for the

00:05:10.103 --> 00:05:11.460
second "Beetlejuice."

00:05:11.460 --> 00:05:13.440
But he, yeah, he was
talking to me every day.

00:05:13.440 --> 00:05:14.460
It was like high school.

00:05:14.460 --> 00:05:15.360
He was just talking to me every day,

00:05:15.360 --> 00:05:16.530
like by our lockers.

00:05:16.530 --> 00:05:17.880
And then he never asked me out.

00:05:17.880 --> 00:05:21.420
And then he went on to
prep the stuff in Vermont,

00:05:21.420 --> 00:05:23.040
and I'm grousing to Tim Burton about how

00:05:23.040 --> 00:05:24.057
the production designer
never asked me out.

00:05:24.057 --> 00:05:25.530
And he said, "Let me see what I can do."

00:05:25.530 --> 00:05:28.320
And I got there first
day of shooting there.

00:05:28.320 --> 00:05:29.370
Bo is off to the side.

00:05:29.370 --> 00:05:30.660
Bo Welch is his name.

00:05:30.660 --> 00:05:33.090
He comes and he goes,
"Um, would like to go,

00:05:33.090 --> 00:05:34.650
the art department's going to a swap meet.

00:05:34.650 --> 00:05:35.919
You want to come with us?"

00:05:35.919 --> 00:05:37.454
(all laugh)
- Wow.

00:05:37.454 --> 00:05:38.310
- It looked like he'd been forced.

00:05:38.310 --> 00:05:40.560
- Really reeling you in. Yeah. (laughs)

00:05:40.560 --> 00:05:42.030
- I guess, but Tim was right.
- Yeah.

00:05:42.030 --> 00:05:42.990
- Made it happen.

00:05:42.990 --> 00:05:44.370
- I love that you got set up.

00:05:44.370 --> 00:05:47.550
This is actually for some
reason tracks for me,

00:05:47.550 --> 00:05:49.680
but like, as a gift for a wedding gift.

00:05:49.680 --> 00:05:51.720
He gave you a tour of the Vatican?

00:05:51.720 --> 00:05:52.553
- Tim did? Yeah.

00:05:52.553 --> 00:05:54.030
A private, no private.

00:05:54.030 --> 00:05:54.960
- Okay, but I'm just like,

00:05:54.960 --> 00:05:57.660
that is the most random wedding
gift I've ever heard of.

00:05:57.660 --> 00:05:59.340
- It's amazing.
- How is it possible?

00:05:59.340 --> 00:06:00.300
- Somebody had given it to him.

00:06:00.300 --> 00:06:02.340
I don't know, it's a
very inside kind of deal.

00:06:02.340 --> 00:06:03.720
(audience laughs)
- What was the-

00:06:03.720 --> 00:06:05.190
- Okay, a priest took us around.

00:06:05.190 --> 00:06:07.080
He got yelled at, at one point.

00:06:07.080 --> 00:06:08.040
I wish I spoke Italian,

00:06:08.040 --> 00:06:09.690
but he was definitely
getting yelled at by another

00:06:09.690 --> 00:06:11.970
priest because we were
in the pope's closet.

00:06:11.970 --> 00:06:13.440
Like looking at garments.

00:06:13.440 --> 00:06:14.400
It was crazy.

00:06:14.400 --> 00:06:15.693
We were in the elevator
- What?

00:06:15.693 --> 00:06:18.900
- that he takes down to St.
Peter's Basilica to do mass.

00:06:18.900 --> 00:06:23.130
We were in this lovely
little, like a tiny museum

00:06:23.130 --> 00:06:26.250
where all the gifts to the
Vatican from every country,

00:06:26.250 --> 00:06:28.590
from every country leader in the world.

00:06:28.590 --> 00:06:29.940
Gifts that had been sent to the Vatican

00:06:29.940 --> 00:06:30.837
since the beginning of the Vatican.

00:06:30.837 --> 00:06:33.630
And this priest is letting us open cases

00:06:33.630 --> 00:06:36.630
and pretending to put a
crown inside Bo's backpack

00:06:36.630 --> 00:06:37.560
taking pictures.

00:06:37.560 --> 00:06:39.180
I wish I had those pictures with me.

00:06:39.180 --> 00:06:40.050
Just crazy.

00:06:40.050 --> 00:06:42.030
- You don't have these pictures?

00:06:42.030 --> 00:06:42.870
- Somewhere I do, yeah.

00:06:42.870 --> 00:06:44.370
- [Kelly] Oh man. That's amazing.

00:06:44.370 --> 00:06:46.505
- Yeah, but what a, yeah,
it was beautiful gift.

00:06:46.505 --> 00:06:47.623
Very generous.
- It's such a random gift.

00:06:47.623 --> 00:06:49.740
A very memorable, I'll give him that.

00:06:49.740 --> 00:06:52.260
- You were rummaging
around the pope's closet?

00:06:52.260 --> 00:06:53.093
- Yes.
(audience laughs)

00:06:53.093 --> 00:06:53.926
- [Kelly] As one does.

00:06:53.926 --> 00:06:55.195
- [Bryce] That's like when you're like

00:06:55.195 --> 00:06:56.028
- I hate to say this out loud. Sorry.

00:06:56.028 --> 00:06:57.150
- going to like real estate or something

00:06:57.150 --> 00:06:58.410
and you're like, "Oh,
I'm just gonna go like,

00:06:58.410 --> 00:07:00.270
walk to these open houses,
and then you're just

00:07:00.270 --> 00:07:01.470
gonna peek around a little bit."

00:07:01.470 --> 00:07:03.122
- I was like, "No, no, yes."

00:07:03.122 --> 00:07:05.160
(all laugh)

00:07:05.160 --> 00:07:06.420
- I will take it. I will.

00:07:06.420 --> 00:07:08.220
Have you, what were
your wedding gifts like?

00:07:08.220 --> 00:07:09.960
Any comparison there?

00:07:09.960 --> 00:07:13.200
- Well, I made, I don't
wanna say I made a mistake,

00:07:13.200 --> 00:07:16.950
I don't regret it, but
I was one of the first

00:07:16.950 --> 00:07:19.680
or the first person of my
group to kind of get married.

00:07:19.680 --> 00:07:22.221
My husband was 24, I was 25.
- Yeah.

00:07:22.221 --> 00:07:24.870
- And at the time we were like, you know,

00:07:24.870 --> 00:07:28.140
vegan, raw foodists, and
very much so you vote

00:07:28.140 --> 00:07:29.430
with your dollar and all that.

00:07:29.430 --> 00:07:33.120
And so it was a wedding
of like in lieu of gifts.

00:07:33.120 --> 00:07:33.953
Right.

00:07:33.953 --> 00:07:34.786
In lieu of gifts.

00:07:34.786 --> 00:07:36.720
Might you donate to or consider something.

00:07:36.720 --> 00:07:37.553
- That's beautiful.

00:07:37.553 --> 00:07:39.210
- It was great, except for now,

00:07:39.210 --> 00:07:41.322
like I still don't have
a proper set of dishes.

00:07:41.322 --> 00:07:42.300
(all laugh)

00:07:42.300 --> 00:07:43.830
Like there are so, we realized this,

00:07:43.830 --> 00:07:47.070
we were like, there are so
many things that you just

00:07:47.070 --> 00:07:49.230
as like a, like a young person, you don't

00:07:49.230 --> 00:07:51.300
know to stock yourself with these things.

00:07:51.300 --> 00:07:53.400
- Yes.
- And it's taken 20 years

00:07:53.400 --> 00:07:54.750
for us to learn how to feed each other.

00:07:54.750 --> 00:07:56.400
Honestly.
- Yes.

00:07:56.400 --> 00:07:57.930
I suffer from this too.
- Yeah.

00:07:57.930 --> 00:07:59.670
- You get paid for jobs though.

00:07:59.670 --> 00:08:01.865
You could probably
afford your dishware now.

00:08:01.865 --> 00:08:02.698
(audience laughs)
- Yeah. so it's like,

